# ============================ ACCOUNTS 1 ============================
LESSONS_A1 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Accounting workspace and the General Ledger", "11", "<p>Open the <b>Accounting</b> workspace. This is the module you will operate for the whole track: the Chart of Accounts, the ledgers and reports, the journal and payment documents, and the settings that govern them. This first module covers the foundation — the Chart of Accounts and the General Ledger — and how to read both.</p><p><b>What a GL Entry is.</b> Every financial event in ZhiftERP is stored as one or more <b>GL Entry</b> rows: posting date, account, debit or credit amount, party where applicable, cost centre, and — always — the <b>voucher</b>: the document that created the row. GL Entries are never edited. Corrections are new entries posted beside the old ones (module 2 covers the procedure). You will not usually open GL Entry records directly; you read them through the <b>General Ledger report</b> (Chapter 6).</p><p><b>The books are written by documents.</b> In ZhiftERP you do not type ledger entries for trading. The trading documents post their own accounting the moment they are submitted:</p><p>• Submitting a <b>Sales Invoice</b> posts the revenue, the VAT, and the customer's debt.<br>• Submitting a <b>Purchase Receipt</b> posts the stock value and the accrual for unbilled goods.<br>• Submitting a <b>Payment Entry</b> posts the bank movement and clears the debt.</p><p>To see this on any submitted document, open it and use the <b>View &gt; Accounting Ledger</b> button: the exact GL rows the document wrote, on one screen. Use this button constantly — it is the fastest teacher in the module.</p><p><b>What this changes about the work.</b> Because documents write the books, the accounts job in ZhiftERP is governing the machine, not typing entries: getting the Chart of Accounts right (Chapters 3-4), knowing what each document posts (Chapter 5), reading the ledger and its reports (Chapters 6-7), and keeping the few genuine manual entries under control (module 2). A business that types Journal Entries for its sales while the Sales Invoice sits unused has switched the system off while paying for it; module 2 covers how to recognise and reverse that habit.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: debits and credits — how to read the two amount columns on every ledger screen. Chapter 3: the Chart of Accounts tree — creating and organising accounts. Chapter 4: the Account Type field and the company default accounts. Chapter 5: the posting map — what each document writes. Chapter 6: the General Ledger and Trial Balance reports. Chapter 7: Receivable and Payable accounts and the party ledgers. Chapter 8: the case — <b>HealthTrade</b>, the pharmaceutical distributor this track follows, setting up its chart. Chapter 9: review and the module's law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Debits and credits: reading the ledger columns", "12", "<p>Every ledger screen in ZhiftERP — the General Ledger report, the Accounting Ledger view, the Journal Entry rows — shows two amount columns: <b>Debit</b> and <b>Credit</b>. Learn the direction table once and every screen becomes readable.</p><p><b>The direction table.</b></p><p>• <b>Assets</b> (bank, debtors, stock, equipment) — debit to increase, credit to decrease.<br>• <b>Expenses</b> (rent, salaries, COGS) — debit to increase.<br>• <b>Liabilities</b> (creditors, VAT payable, loans) — credit to increase.<br>• <b>Income</b> (sales) — credit to increase.<br>• <b>Equity</b> (capital, retained earnings) — credit to increase.</p><p>Mnemonic: <b>DEAD</b> — Debits increase Expenses, Assets, Drawings; everything else grows by credit. Every document's rows balance: total debits equal total credits, always. The system enforces this; you never see an unbalanced posting.</p><p><b>Three documents, read on screen.</b> Post these in a test company and open <b>View &gt; Accounting Ledger</b> on each:</p><p>1. <b>A Sales Invoice for ₦107,500</b> (₦100,000 + 7.5% VAT): Debtors debited ₦107,500 (the customer now owes it), Sales credited ₦100,000 (income), VAT Payable credited ₦7,500 (money held for FIRS, not income — module 5 covers the tax accounts).<br>2. <b>The customer's Payment Entry:</b> Bank debited ₦107,500, Debtors credited ₦107,500 — the debt became cash.<br>3. <b>A Purchase Receipt at ₦940,000:</b> Stock In Hand debited ₦940,000, <b>Stock Received But Not Billed</b> credited ₦940,000 — the system records that you hold goods you have not yet been billed for. Chapter 5 completes this thread when the Purchase Invoice arrives.</p><p><b>Three reads the table gives you.</b></p><p>• <b>The wrong-side balance.</b> Each account normally carries one side. A customer showing a <b>credit</b> balance in the Accounts Receivable report means you owe them — usually a payment that was never allocated to its invoices. Wrong-side balances are always worth opening.<br>• <b>The growth read.</b> Credits growing a Sales account is trade; debits growing it are returns and credit notes — check them when large.<br>• <b>The other side.</b> Every posting has a counterpart row. When an entry looks wrong in the General Ledger report, find its voucher and open the Accounting Ledger view — the counterpart row usually names the mistake.</p><p>Practical rule: when a balance confuses you, say it in the table's terms — which account, which side, grew by what — then open the entries. Confusion rarely survives the two steps.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Chart of Accounts screen", "12", "<p>Open <b>Accounting &gt; Chart of Accounts</b>. The screen shows a tree: bold rows are <b>group</b> accounts (folders for reading), and the rest are <b>ledger</b> accounts — the leaves that take postings. Posting to a group is refused by the system. This chapter is how to build and maintain the tree.</p><p><b>Creating an account.</b> Hover a group row and click <b>Add Child</b>. The dialog asks for:</p><p>• <b>Account Name</b> — plain language that will read well in every report for years: <i>Generator Running Costs</i>, not <i>Misc Exp 2</i>.<br>• <b>Account Number</b> — optional; use it if the business's reporting tradition numbers accounts. Keep gaps for insertion and never reuse a number.<br>• <b>Is Group</b> — tick to create a folder; leave clear for a posting account. A group cannot take postings; a leaf cannot have children. To convert one to the other later, the account must have no entries.<br>• <b>Account Type</b> — Chapter 4; the field that wires the account into the machinery.</p><p>Every account belongs to one of the fixed <b>root types</b> — Asset, Liability, Equity, Income, Expense — inherited from its position in the tree.</p><p><b>Design from the reading, not the listing.</b> The default chart the setup wizard installs is a workable skeleton. Tailor it in one sitting:</p><p>1. Write down the owner's monthly questions — what did we make, what did it cost, where did the money go. These define the income and expense groups.<br>2. Add what the statements and the audit need. These define the balance-sheet shape.<br>3. Apply the reading test to every proposed account: <b>an account earns its existence when someone will read its line separately.</b> Three bank accounts are three leaves because three reconciliations will happen. Eleven micro-splits of office expenses that nobody reads separately are clutter — collapse them.</p><p><b>Accounts versus dimensions.</b> Do not create <i>Rent - Ibadan</i>, <i>Rent - Akure</i>. Branch and line distinctions belong in the <b>Cost Center</b> field on postings (module 6), not in account names. One account, <i>Rent</i>; the branch is a filter. The test: opening a new branch should not add a single account.</p><p><b>Maintaining the tree.</b></p><p>• <b>Renaming</b> is safe: open the account and use <b>Rename</b> — history follows the account.<br>• <b>Moving or merging</b> accounts changes how history reads: plan it, do it at a period boundary, document it. The Chart of Accounts screen allows drag-free re-parenting through the account's Parent Account field.<br>• <b>Keep creation rights narrow.</b> New accounts are proposed against the reading test, not created per whim. Chapter 9's sprawl pattern is what unrestricted creation produces.</p><p>Practical rule: finish the design sitting with the one-line test — every leaf has a one-sentence answer to <i>who reads this line and why</i>. Retire any account that fails it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Account Type and the company defaults", "12", "<p>Two accounts can sit side by side in the tree and behave completely differently, because of one field: <b>Account Type</b>. Names are for people; the type is what the machinery reads.</p><p><b>The types that matter, and what each switches on.</b></p><p>• <b>Bank</b> and <b>Cash</b> — the account joins the payment machinery: Modes of Payment can point at it, and Bank-type accounts appear in the <b>Bank Reconciliation</b> tool (module 3). A real bank account created without the Bank type is invisible to the reconciliation that proves it.<br>• <b>Receivable</b> and <b>Payable</b> — every posting to the account <b>requires a Party</b> (Customer or Supplier). This is what creates the per-party ledgers and the Accounts Receivable / Accounts Payable reports (Chapter 7). An invoice pointed at a non-Receivable account leaves the machinery that ages balances and prints statements.<br>• <b>Stock</b> — reserved for the perpetual-inventory postings: Stock In Hand, Stock Received But Not Billed, the adjustment accounts. The system blocks Journal Entries against Stock-type accounts while perpetual inventory is on (module 2, Chapter 6 explains why).<br>• <b>Tax</b> — the VAT and WHT accounts the tax templates post to; typed so the tax reports can find them.<br>• <b>Fixed Asset, Depreciation, Accumulated Depreciation</b> — the asset module's accounts (module 7).<br>• <b>Round Off</b> and <b>Temporary</b> — the system's designated accounts for rounding differences and opening balances.</p><p><b>The company defaults.</b> Open the <b>Company</b> record (Accounting &gt; Company) and review its account defaults: Default Receivable Account, Default Payable Account, Default Cash and Bank Accounts, Round Off Account and Cost Center, Exchange Gain/Loss Account, Stock Received But Not Billed, Stock Adjustment Account, and the rest. Every document that does not name an account explicitly falls back to these. <b>A wrong default mis-posts every document that relies on it, silently, from day one.</b> Verify the defaults at setup and re-verify after any chart restructuring.</p><p><b>Three working rules.</b></p><p>1. <b>Set the type at creation.</b> Retyping an account that has history is a serious event — a receivable that spent a year untyped has a year of party-less entries no toggle repairs.<br>2. <b>When a posting misbehaves, check the type first.</b> The invoice that refuses a Party, the bank missing from the reconciliation list — open the account record and read its Account Type before touching anything else.<br>3. <b>Audit the types annually.</b> Every Bank-type account against the real bank list; the Receivable/Payable accounts against the company defaults; the Round Off account against its intended leaf. Thirty minutes; it removes the whole class of rule-2 errors.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The posting map: what each document writes", "14", "<p>This chapter is the reference card for the track: exactly which GL rows each document posts on submit. Verify each one in a test company with <b>View &gt; Accounting Ledger</b>.</p><p><b>Sales Invoice.</b> Debit Debtors (with the Customer as Party) for the gross; credit the income account per row; credit the tax accounts per the Sales Taxes and Charges template. If <b>Update Stock</b> is ticked (the POS-style flow), the invoice also does the delivery's work: credit Stock In Hand, debit Cost of Goods Sold.</p><p><b>Delivery Note.</b> Credit Stock In Hand at the item's valuation rate; debit Cost of Goods Sold. <b>No revenue posts here.</b> Delivery moves value; invoicing books revenue — which is why a period needs both its deliveries and its invoices in before its margin is true (module 7's cut-off).</p><p><b>Payment Entry (Receive).</b> Debit the bank or cash account (from the Mode of Payment); credit Debtors with the Party, allocated against invoices in the <b>References</b> table. Deductions ride in the same entry's Deductions table — WHT withheld by the customer debits the WHT receivable account there (module 5).</p><p><b>Purchase Receipt.</b> Debit Stock In Hand at valuation (rate plus landed costs); credit <b>Stock Received But Not Billed</b> — the accrual that records goods held but not yet billed. Diagnostic worth memorising: the SRBNB account's balance <b>is</b> the queue of receipts awaiting purchase invoices, in naira. If it is old and large, invoices are not being processed.</p><p><b>Purchase Invoice.</b> Credit Creditors (with the Supplier as Party); debit Stock Received But Not Billed, clearing the accrual; debit the input-VAT account per the taxes template. A service invoice with no receipt debits the expense account directly. WHT you deduct from the supplier is applied through the Tax Withholding Category and posts to WHT Payable when the invoice or payment is processed (module 5).</p><p><b>Stock Entry.</b> Material Issue: credit Stock In Hand, debit the expense account named on the entry. Material Receipt: debit Stock In Hand, credit the difference account. Material Transfer: stock accounts only — nothing touches the P&amp;L.</p><p><b>Stock Reconciliation.</b> Debit or credit Stock In Hand against the adjustment account set on the entry — the count difference landing in the P&amp;L where policy directed it.</p><p><b>Journal Entry.</b> Whatever its rows say — which is exactly why module 2 exists.</p><p><b>Three ways to use the map.</b></p><p>1. <b>Verify it live at setup.</b> Post one small real document of each kind; read the Accounting Ledger view against this chapter. A wrong default or template is caught in an hour instead of inherited by a month.<br>2. <b>Diagnose backwards.</b> A strange GL row names its voucher; the map says what that document should have written; the difference locates the fault.<br>3. <b>Answer forwards.</b> \"Why did this account move?\" — the map names the documents that write it; the General Ledger report filtered by account lists the period's instances.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The General Ledger and Trial Balance reports", "12", "<p>Two reports carry most of the reading in this track. Learn their filters and the drill-down and every accounts question becomes a lookup.</p><p><b>The General Ledger report</b> (Accounting &gt; General Ledger). The filters that matter:</p><p>• <b>Company / From Date / To Date</b> — always set first. The report shows the opening balance, every entry in range, and the closing balance.<br>• <b>Account</b> — one account's full story: use it for \"how did this balance get here\".<br>• <b>Party Type + Party</b> — one customer or supplier across accounts: their statement on screen.<br>• <b>Voucher No</b> — one document's complete footprint: the posting map, verified live.<br>• <b>Group By</b> — Group by Voucher to read documents; Group by Account to read balances.<br>• Cost Center and dimension filters — module 6's lenses.</p><p><b>The drill-down.</b> Every number in every financial report links downward: statement line → account → GL rows → the voucher → its attachments. Click through it rather than exporting: the disputed figure is opened live in the meeting; the strange balance is walked to its documents the same day. Teach this habit in the first month — a business whose managers drill down argues from documents.</p><p><b>The Trial Balance report</b> (Accounting &gt; Trial Balance). One row per account: opening debit/credit, period debit and credit, closing balance. It always balances — the posting engine guarantees it — so read it for anomalies, not for its total:</p><p>1. <b>Accounts that should be zero and are not</b> — Stock Received But Not Billed aging (unprocessed purchase invoices), a temporary or suspense account with residents, a Round Off account trending upward (a symptom, not rounding).<br>2. <b>Balances on the wrong side</b> — a credit-balance customer (an unallocated payment), a debit-balance VAT Payable.<br>3. <b>Accounts nobody recognises</b> — Chapter 9's pattern; investigate to the entries, then merge, retire, or document.</p><p><b>The monthly scan, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. Run the Trial Balance for the month.<br>2. Flag the three anomaly kinds above.<br>3. For each flag, open the General Ledger report filtered to that account and read the entries to their vouchers.<br>4. Fix what has a fix (allocation, correction by module 2's rules) and note what needs a decision.</p><p>Ten minutes when done monthly. It is the reason the year-end close (module 7) holds no surprises.</p><p>Practical rule: never leave a wrong-side balance or an unexplained account for the close to find. The voucher that explains it is four clicks away today and an excavation in six months.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Receivable and Payable accounts: the party ledgers", "12", "<p>Debtors is <b>one account</b> in the chart, but because every posting to it carries a Party (Chapter 4's type rule), the system maintains a ledger per customer inside it. This chapter is that structure and the check that keeps it trustworthy.</p><p><b>The three faces of the same entries.</b></p><p>• The <b>General Ledger report filtered by Party</b> is the customer's statement — every invoice, payment and credit note in one run.<br>• The <b>Accounts Receivable report</b> (Accounting &gt; Accounts Receivable) ages every customer's open items into buckets — the collections worklist. Accounts Payable mirrors it for suppliers.<br>• The <b>Debtors account balance</b> in the Trial Balance is, by construction, the sum of the parties' balances.</p><p>The pattern: the control account is the total; the parties are the detail; they agree because they are the same GL rows read three ways.</p><p><b>How the agreement breaks.</b> The tie holds automatically until someone posts around it:</p><p>• A Journal Entry row against Debtors <b>without a Party</b> — the system requires a Party on Receivable/Payable accounts, but imported entries, migrations, and permission overrides can slip past. The result: naira in the control that belong to no customer; the AR report's total no longer equals the account.<br>• A balance \"corrected\" at the account level while the party detail stands.<br>• A migration that loaded one opening total per control instead of per-party, per-invoice detail (module 4 covers the loading rules).</p><p><b>The monthly tie check, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. Run the Trial Balance; note the Debtors closing balance.<br>2. Run the Accounts Receivable report as at the same date; note its grand total.<br>3. They must agree to the kobo. Repeat for Creditors against Accounts Payable.<br>4. On any difference: run the General Ledger report on the control account filtered to <b>Voucher Type = Journal Entry</b>. The offending entry is almost always there, in minutes.</p><p><b>What an intact tie buys.</b> The ageing report is believed, so collections and the payment run work from it. Customer statements print from the party ledger without fear. The auditor samples parties, finds the control every time, and the confirmation exercise ends early. The balance sheet's receivables line is a total with hundreds of defensible components.</p><p>Practical rule: the allocation habits taught at the sales and purchasing desks — payments allocated to invoices in the References table, credit notes applied, advances matched — are all tie maintenance from this chair. Module 4 turns them into a standing monthly routine.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's chart is born", "13", "<p><b>HealthTrade</b> is the pharmaceutical distributor this track follows — three branches, an in-house accountant, a new ZhiftERP system. This chapter is the afternoon its chart was set up, using Chapters 1-7 in order.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the reading list (Chapter 3).</b> Before touching the tree, the MD's monthly questions are written down: sales and margin (eventually by branch — noted for module 6), the running costs read separately (rent, people, power, logistics), and the balance-sheet picture (banks by account, stock, debtors, creditors, the tax accounts). The accountant adds the statement and audit needs. Fifteen questions become the chart's specification.</p><p><b>Step 2 — tailoring the tree (Chapters 3-4).</b> On the Chart of Accounts screen, against the list:</p><p>• Three real banks become three <b>Bank-type</b> leaves under Bank Accounts, named with bank and account identity: <i>GTB Current - 0123</i> style — three reconciliations coming in module 3.<br>• Debtors and Creditors confirmed as the typed control accounts, and the <b>Company record's defaults</b> walked: Default Receivable and Payable, Round Off Account, Stock Received But Not Billed — two are corrected on the spot (the Round Off account pointed at a proper leaf, and one Mode of Payment mapped to the wrong bank).<br>• The tax accounts named plainly: VAT Payable, Input VAT, WHT Payable, WHT Receivable — all typed Tax.<br>• Eleven micro-splits of administrative expenses collapsed to four lines someone will actually read.<br>• The branch question answered the right way: <b>no</b> per-branch account copies; the branch will be a Cost Center (module 6). One chart, one language.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the posting map verified live (Chapter 5).</b> One small real document of each kind is posted and its <b>View &gt; Accounting Ledger</b> read against the map: a test invoice (Debtors gross, Sales net, VAT Payable — the ₦107,500 pattern read aloud), a Purchase Receipt (the SRBNB accrual explained: <i>this balance is your unbilled receipts queue — watch it clear when the invoice books</i>), a delivery's COGS row tied to the valuation rate, a Payment Entry allocated in its References table and re-read in the party ledger. The hour does what Chapter 5 promises: the wrong defaults are caught before a month inherits them.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the first reads (Chapters 6-7).</b> The drill-down is demonstrated: statement line → account → GL rows → voucher, four clicks. The first Trial Balance scan finds one anomaly — a small credit balance on a customer — drilled in the General Ledger report to an unallocated go-live payment, allocated the same day through Payment Reconciliation. Two monthly routines are diarised: the ten-minute Trial Balance scan, and the tie check (Trial Balance Debtors against the AR report total, to the kobo). The accountant's note at the end of the afternoon: <i>the chart answers my questions instead of the software's.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the first law", "11", "<p>The recurring chart-and-ledger failures. Symptom, cause, fix — then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — chart sprawl.</b> Symptom: four hundred accounts for an eleven-person business; every report a scroll; nobody can say what half the accounts are for. Cause: accounts created per whim — one per project, branch, or mood (Chapter 3). Fix: a consolidation sitting — balances merged by documented entries into accounts that are read, unused accounts disabled, creation rights narrowed, and the branch/line distinctions moved to Cost Centers.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the suspense squatter.</b> Symptom: a suspense or miscellaneous account with a large, aging, growing balance. Cause: the parking account used as a destination for everything unclassified (Chapters 3, 6). Fix: suspense swept to zero monthly as a close-checklist line, each item classified to its true account, and the source gaps fixed — a default, a template, or training.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the untyped account.</b> Symptom: a bank missing from the Bank Reconciliation tool; an invoice refusing a Party; receivables that cannot age. Cause: accounts created with names but without the right Account Type (Chapter 4). Fix: type at creation as a rule, retyping-with-history treated as the serious event it is, and the annual type audit.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the party-less posting.</b> Symptom: the Trial Balance's Debtors figure disagreeing with the Accounts Receivable report's total. Cause: entries posted to control accounts without a Party — imports, migrations, permission overrides (Chapter 7). Fix: reconcile the divergence once (the Journal Entry filter finds it), install module 2's journal rules, and run the monthly tie check afterwards.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the account nobody can explain.</b> Symptom: the Trial Balance line that draws blank looks in the meeting. Cause: the chart accumulated instead of designed (Chapter 3). Fix: the annual chart walk — each unexplained account read to its entries in the General Ledger report, then merged, disabled, or documented. Deliverable: every line has its one-sentence explanation.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the direction table and DEAD; read the three worked documents from Chapter 2 in the Accounting Ledger view; state the reading test and the account-versus-dimension rule; name the Account Types and what each switches on, and where the company defaults live; recite the posting map document by document, including the SRBNB diagnostic; run the General Ledger report by account, party, and voucher, and the monthly Trial Balance scan; and perform the tie check with its Journal Entry filter.</p><p><b>The first law: the books are written by documents, into a chart designed for reading.</b> Module 2 opens the one document that writes the ledger directly — the Journal Entry — and the rules that keep it safe.</p>"),
]

