# ============================ ACCOUNTS 2 ============================
LESSONS_A2 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Journal Entry doctype", "11", "<p>Open <b>Accounting &gt; Journal Entry &gt; New</b>. This is the one document whose job is writing the General Ledger directly — any accounts, any amounts, whatever its rows say. It exists because real accounting needs it monthly (Chapter 3 lists the lawful uses). It is dangerous because of what it bypasses. This module teaches the form, the uses, and the fences.</p><p><b>What the Journal Entry bypasses.</b> Every trading document carries machinery around its GL posting:</p><p>• The Sales Invoice maintains the party ledger, feeds the tax registers, and ages into the Accounts Receivable report.<br>• The Purchase Receipt writes the stock ledger beside the money.<br>• The Payment Entry allocates against invoices in its References table.</p><p>The Journal Entry carries <b>none</b> of that. It writes bare GL rows. Every naira it posts has skipped the systems that make totals decomposable — the party tie, the stock-to-accounts agreement, the tax registers, the payment allocation. That is not a flaw: the Journal Entry is specialised for events that have no trading document (rent accruing, depreciation charging). But used where a document exists, it fixes the GL's number while breaking every detail beneath it — silently. The Trial Balance still balances; the statements still print; the damage surfaces weeks later as a tie check that will not agree.</p><p><b>The module's rule, stated now.</b> <b>The journal is the exception and the document is the rule: what a document can do, a document must do.</b> Sales are invoiced. Purchases are billed. Stock moves by stock documents. Payments are Payment Entries. The Journal Entry takes what remains — Chapter 3's list, under Chapter 7's controls.</p><p><b>The habit to watch for.</b> Accountants trained on manual books often journalise everything — some systems in the wild are a GL with thousands of Journal Entries and no trading documents at all. The fix is transferring the fluency, not deriding it: each recurring journal examined for the document that should replace it, corrections routed through Chapter 5's procedures, and the <b>monthly Journal Entry count</b> watched as a health figure — open the Journal Entry list, filter by month, read the number. Lawful traffic at a trading business is small; a large or growing count is itself a finding.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the form, field by field. Chapter 3: the lawful list. Chapter 4: accruals and prepayments, with the exact procedures. Chapter 5: corrections. Chapter 6: the forbidden list and the system's fences. Chapter 7: permissions, templates, and the monthly review. Chapter 8: HealthTrade's manual layer. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Journal Entry form, field by field", "12", "<p>The controls in this module live in the form's own fields. Walk them in order on a new Journal Entry.</p><p><b>Entry Type.</b> The first field: Journal Entry, Bank Entry, Cash Entry, Credit Note, Debit Note, Contra Entry, Depreciation Entry, and others. The type does two jobs: some machinery work (Contra Entries are how movements between your own bank and cash accounts are recorded — module 3 uses them), and review work — the monthly review filters by type, and a Bank Entry saved as a plain Journal Entry hides from its reviewer. Choose it correctly every time.</p><p><b>Posting Date.</b> When the entry speaks. Rules: honest dates always (the March accrual is dated in March); closed periods are respected (the freeze settings in module 8 block postings into sealed months); and accrual reversals are dated the 1st of the following month (Chapter 4's procedure).</p><p><b>The Accounting Entries table.</b> One row per account touched:</p><p>• <b>Account</b> — a leaf account; the system refuses groups.<br>• <b>Party Type / Party</b> — mandatory when the account is Receivable or Payable type. The form will not save a Debtors row without its Customer.<br>• <b>Debit / Credit</b> — one side per row.<br>• <b>Cost Center</b> — required on income and expense rows once module 6's validation is on. An expense journal must still say whose expense.<br>• <b>Reference Type / Name</b> — when a row settles against an existing invoice, name it here so the allocation lands.</p><p><b>The Difference field.</b> Below the table, the form shows total debit, total credit, and the difference. <b>The entry will not submit until the difference is zero.</b> A journal that seems to need one side only is a misdiagnosis — the other side exists and has not been found. Parking the difference in a suspense account is not the answer (Chapter 9, Pattern 2).</p><p><b>Reference Number and Reference Date.</b> The external anchor: the bank advice, the payroll summary, the agreement clause. Fill them whenever such paper exists — module 3 requires them on every Bank Entry.</p><p><b>User Remark.</b> The story field, and the most strictly enforced rule in this module: <b>every Journal Entry narrates itself</b> — what happened, why this entry, what evidence supports it. Trading documents are their own explanation; a journal's explanation lives only here. A journal with an empty remark is unusable six months later — nobody, including its author, can say why it exists. No exceptions; templates (Chapter 7) carry their remarks pre-written, so routine traffic costs nothing.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Correct Entry Type, honest Posting Date, rows with parties and cost centres, references named where rows settle documents, the Difference at zero, external references attached, and a story in User Remark — every time.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The lawful list", "12", "<p>The discipline only holds if the lawful uses are named rather than negotiated case by case. This is the list — a trading business's legitimate Journal Entry traffic:</p><p><b>1. Accruals and prepayments.</b> Expenses spread to the months that consumed them; income deferred to the months that earned it. The largest lawful category, monthly by nature — Chapter 4 gives the procedures.</p><p><b>2. Payroll.</b> The salary journal from the payroll register: debit salary expense (by cost centre), credit the net-pay liability (cleared by the payment run), credit the statutory accounts — PAYE, pension, other deductions — each a liability holding money withheld for someone else. Where the payroll module posts this automatically, read the generated entry against the posting map once anyway. Module 5 covers the Nigerian statutory detail.</p><p><b>3. Depreciation.</b> Debit depreciation expense, credit accumulated depreciation — generated by the fixed-asset module where it runs (module 7), a governed monthly Journal Entry where it does not yet.</p><p><b>4. Bank charges and bank-side events.</b> The charges the statement reveals: debit bank charges, credit the bank — entered as <b>Bank Entries</b> with the statement as the Reference, and only ever from the reconciliation (module 3), never from memory.</p><p><b>5. Opening balances.</b> The go-live event: governed, evidenced, approved, loaded once. The critical rule: opening debtors and creditors load as <b>per-party, per-invoice detail</b> — through the Opening Invoice Creation Tool or per-document opening entries — never one total posted to a control account. The tie must be born intact. Module 4 details the migration procedure.</p><p><b>6. Owner and inter-entity movements.</b> Capital introduced, drawings, director loans, movements between related entities — real events with no trading document. Journalised with the evidence referenced, parties where applicable, and the tax and legal shape decided by the accountant.</p><p><b>7. Corrections.</b> Lawful only under Chapter 5's procedures.</p><p><b>The two tests.</b> Whether a proposed journal belongs on this list:</p><p>1. <b>The document test.</b> Does a trading document exist for this event? If yes, the journal is forbidden regardless of convenience — the sale, the purchase, the stock move, and the payment all have documents.<br>2. <b>The evidence test.</b> Can the journal name its paper in the Reference fields — the agreement, the statement, the summary, the schedule? A journal that cannot reference evidence is an assertion, not an entry.</p><p>Practical rule: the lawful list for a given business fits on one page, and most of it recurs monthly. Chapter 7 turns that page into templates — after which anything outside the templates is, by construction, visible and reviewable.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Accruals and prepayments: the procedures", "13", "<p>The largest lawful category, with the exact procedures. The principle in one sentence first: the P&amp;L should state what a month <b>consumed</b>, not what it happened to pay — payment dates are bank facts; expense dates are consumption facts. Without this, margins lurch with payment calendars and every monthly report reads noise.</p><p><b>Prepayment procedure.</b> HealthTrade pays annual rent of ₦2,400,000 in January.</p><p>1. <b>The payment</b> posts to an asset: Payment Entry (or the rent invoice) debits <b>Prepaid Rent</b> ₦2,400,000, credits Bank. Eleven months of future occupancy is a thing the business owns.<br>2. <b>The monthly consumption journal</b>, twelve times: debit Rent Expense ₦200,000, credit Prepaid Rent ₦200,000.<br>3. <b>Automate it:</b> create the first consumption entry, then set up <b>Auto Repeat</b> on it (monthly, twelve occurrences) — or save it as a Journal Entry Template and post from the template each close. The User Remark is written once and rides every month.</p><p>Result: each month's P&amp;L carries ₦200,000 of rent; the Prepaid Rent balance at any date <b>is</b> the occupancy still owed — a balance-sheet line that means something. The same shape serves insurance, licences, and subscriptions. (For deferred revenue and expense driven from items, the system can generate these schedules automatically — module 8's settings walk covers enabling it.)</p><p><b>Accrual procedure.</b> The mirror. March consumes ₦180,000 of diesel and power; the bills arrive in April.</p><p>1. <b>The close journal</b>, dated in March: debit Utilities Expense ₦180,000, credit <b>Accrued Expenses</b> (a liability). March's P&amp;L now tells March's truth.<br>2. <b>The reversal:</b> after submitting, use the <b>Reverse Journal Entry</b> action and date the reversal April 1st — the system builds the exact mirror and links the two.<br>3. <b>April's actual bills</b> post through their normal Purchase Invoices. The reversal has already cleared the estimate, so the actuals land clean — no double count.</p><p>The <b>estimate → reverse → actual</b> rhythm is the monthly craft. The close-checklist question that drives it: <i>which consumptions have no bills yet?</i></p><p><b>The income side.</b> Cash received ahead of earning is not income — it is a liability (unearned income) until earned, then a journal moves it to income as the earning happens. Deposits and retainers follow this shape; module 4 covers capturing customer advances properly at the Payment Entry.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Prepayment: pay into the asset, consume monthly by Auto Repeat or template. Accrual: estimate at close, Reverse Journal Entry dated the 1st, let the actual documents land. Income received early: liability first. Build all of it as templates; run it monthly, never annually.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Corrections", "13", "<p>Mistakes happen — the wrong account, the wrong amount, the wrong month. What separates clean books from archaeology is how corrections are made. Four rules, with their procedures.</p><p><b>Rule 1 — history is never edited.</b> A submitted entry is a permanent record. Corrections are new entries that reverse and restate, standing beside the mistake, telling the full story: error, discovery, repair. Even where permissions technically allow cancelling and amending a posted journal, prefer the visible correction — an edited past is a ledger that lies about its own history.</p><p><b>Rule 2 — reverse and repost, the procedure.</b></p><p>1. Open the wrong Journal Entry and use <b>Reverse Journal Entry</b> — the system creates the exact mirror, linked to the original.<br>2. Create the fresh entry with the correct accounts and amounts.<br>3. Cross-reference all three in their User Remarks: <i>reversal of JV-0231, expense posted to wrong account; see JV-0233 for correct posting.</i></p><p>The GL then reads mistake — mirror — truth: three linked entries any reviewer reconstructs in a minute.</p><p><b>Rule 3 — documents are corrected as documents.</b> The most-violated rule. When the error lives in a trading document — the invoice with the wrong customer, the receipt with the wrong quantity — the correction happens at the <b>document</b>:</p><p>• <b>Cancel and Amend</b> where the document chain allows it — the Cancel button, then Amend, producing the corrected document under a new revision.<br>• <b>The returns and note machinery</b> where trade has moved on — the <b>Return / Credit Note</b> button on the Sales Invoice, the Purchase Return on the receipt, the Debit Note on the bill. These correct the balance, the stock, the tax register, and the party ledger in one instrument.<br>• <b>Never</b> a Journal Entry patching the GL around the document. The patch fixes the balance while the document still tells the old story — and the party ledger, the stock ledger, and the tax register now all disagree with a GL that looks right.</p><p>The diagnostic that follows: <b>a tie that will not reconcile almost always has a Journal Entry in it</b> — and the fastest audit in this track is the General Ledger report on the control, stock, tax, and bank accounts filtered to Voucher Type = Journal Entry, where honest traffic is nearly zero.</p><p><b>Rule 4 — the period decides the date.</b> An error found in an open month is corrected in that month, honest-dated. An error found after the close is corrected in the <b>current</b> month, with remarks naming the original period and entry — sealed months are not reopened for corrections (module 7 covers the close; module 8 the freeze settings).</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Never edit; Reverse Journal Entry plus a fresh posting with cross-references; documents corrected by Cancel/Amend or the note machinery, never by journal; and corrections dated by the period rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The forbidden list and the system's fences", "13", "<p>Some journals are not merely governed — they are forbidden, because each one breaks machinery that cannot report its own breakage. The system fences some of them itself; the rest are policy. Learn each with <i>what breaks</i>.</p><p><b>1. Revenue journals replacing invoices.</b> Debit Debtors, credit Sales, no Sales Invoice. Breaks: the VAT register (output tax untracked — a compliance failure), the customer's document trail (a balance with no invoice to allocate, dispute, or print on a statement), collections (nothing ages properly), and e-invoicing obligations where they apply. The purchase-side twin — expense journals replacing Purchase Invoices — breaks input VAT, WHT capture, and the supplier ledger the same way.</p><p><b>2. Journals to Stock-type accounts.</b> The system's own fence: with perpetual inventory on, <b>the form refuses Journal Entry rows against Stock accounts</b> and raises an error naming the stock documents to use instead. The fence exists because a journal here moves the GL while the stock ledger stands still — the two diverge by exactly the journal's amount, permanently. If stock value is wrong, the error is in a stock document — a receipt's rate, a missing entry, a reconciliation — fix it there.</p><p><b>3. Journals to control accounts.</b> Without a Party the form refuses the row. Even <b>with</b> a Party, treat control-account journals with suspicion: the events they usually claim — write-offs, discounts, settlements — have proper instruments (Credit/Debit Notes, Payment Entry deductions, module 4's provisioning) that carry the tax and ledger consequences the bare journal skips. Working rule: control-account journals are rare, party-carrying, and second-approved.</p><p><b>4. Journals to tax accounts outside the filing routine.</b> Breaks: the register agreement — a VAT Payable that no longer equals what the invoice register says. Tax accounts move by documents and by the filing-and-remittance entries (module 5). The journal that forces the account to match a spreadsheet is backwards: reconcile the register, find the divergence, fix its document.</p><p><b>5. Journals to bank accounts outside real bank events.</b> Breaks: the reconciliation — the GL bank balance acquires a row the statement will never show, an unexplained item forever. Bank accounts move by Payment Entries, Contra Entries, and the reconciliation's own charge entries — nothing else.</p><p><b>The shared shape.</b> Every item on this list is the same mistake: <b>a total adjusted around its details.</b> The GL looks right; the machinery beneath disagrees; the disagreement surfaces later, further from its cause. When a standing agreement will not reconcile, run the Journal Entry filter on that account family first — it is the fastest diagnosis in this track.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Permissions, templates, and the monthly review", "12", "<p>Direct GL access is a dangerous power, and it gets the standard treatment: named holders, thresholds, templates, and a monthly review.</p><p><b>Who may journal.</b> In Role Permissions, the Journal Entry doctype's create/submit rights go to <b>the accountant and one named backstop</b> — two people, not two departments. Warehouse operators, sales staff, and branch managers do not hold the pen; their corrections route through documents and requests. A journal can move any naira anywhere; the day everyone holds the pen, every fence in Chapter 6 becomes a suggestion.</p><p><b>Thresholds and second approval.</b> Configure a submission <b>Workflow</b> on Journal Entry:</p><p>• Value thresholds route large journals to a second sign-off, even between the two named holders.<br>• Certain categories are second-approved regardless of value: anything touching control accounts, opening and closing entries, and corrections above the materiality line.<br>• The threshold numbers are the business's own policy, written down like every other control number.</p><p><b>Templates for the recurring set.</b> Build the lawful monthly traffic once as <b>Journal Entry Templates</b> (and Auto Repeat where the amounts are fixed): accounts pre-wired, cost centres pre-set, remarks pre-written, amounts updated per period. Two payoffs:</p><p>1. The template list is the manual layer's honest census — a trading business's recurring journals fit on one page.<br>2. Everything outside the templates is, by construction, an event: visible and reviewable. The review question changes from <i>are these thousand journals right?</i> to <i>the recurring set ran; what are these six others?</i></p><p><b>The monthly review, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. Open the Journal Entry list; filter to the month; note the <b>count</b> (the standing health figure).<br>2. Read each entry against five questions: <b>author, accounts, direction, date, story</b>.<br>3. Bounce any storyless entry back for its User Remark.<br>4. Read the aggregate: the count trending up (the machine being declined), the same correction repeating (an upstream fault to fix at source), one author's entries clustering at period-end (a conversation).</p><p>Ten to twenty minutes at a healthy business, because the count is small.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Two named holders in Role Permissions; a Workflow carrying thresholds and category approvals; the recurring set as one page of templates with Auto Repeat; and the monthly five-question review, both per-entry and in aggregate.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's manual layer", "13", "<p>The month HealthTrade's manual layer was built, and the three tests that proved it.</p><p><b>The recurring set built (Chapters 3-4, 7).</b> The accountant and the implementation team census the genuine journal traffic and build it in an afternoon:</p><p>• <b>Rent:</b> the ₦2.4m January payment into Prepaid Rent; the ₦200,000 monthly consumption entry created once and put on <b>Auto Repeat</b>, twelve occurrences, remark pre-written.<br>• <b>Payroll:</b> the salary journal template from the payroll register — expense by cost centre, net-pay liability, PAYE and pension custody accounts. Module 5's detail noted.<br>• <b>Utilities accrual:</b> the month-end estimate template. The estimate-reverse-actual rhythm is demonstrated live across March and April: March's ₦180,000 accrual submitted, <b>Reverse Journal Entry</b> dated April 1st, April's actual bills posting through Purchase Invoices, the net landing clean. The accountant, seeing the linked reversal work, builds two more accrual templates unprompted.<br>• <b>Depreciation:</b> a governed monthly template for now; the fixed-asset module noted for module 7.</p><p>The census closes at <b>seven recurring templates — one page</b>, as Chapter 7 promised.</p><p><b>A correction done right (Chapter 5).</b> Week three: February's generator maintenance — ₦340,000 — is found posted to Repairs when policy wants it in Generator Running Costs, the line the MD reads separately. The temptation to cancel-and-amend the old journal quietly is declined out loud, as teaching. Instead: <b>Reverse Journal Entry</b> on the original (linked automatically), a fresh posting to the right account, and all three User Remarks cross-referencing. The drill-down from the MD's generator line now tells the whole story in four clicks. The accountant's comment: <i>the books remember the mistake and the repair — that is what makes them believable.</i></p><p><b>A forbidden journal refused (Chapter 6).</b> Week four: a branch manager calls — a customer disputes ₦86,000; <i>just adjust the debtor and we'll sort it later.</i> The refusal is the fence working: a journal to Debtors would move the control while no document tells the customer's ledger why; the printed statement would show a naked adjustment and the dispute would lose its paper trail. The routing instead: the dispute is investigated, resolves as a pricing error on one line, and a <b>Credit Note</b> is issued from the original invoice's Return button — correcting the balance, the VAT register, and the customer's statement in one instrument. The manager gets the outcome; the books get it the right way.</p><p><b>Month end (Chapter 7).</b> The first monthly review runs from the filtered Journal Entry list: the seven templates fired on schedule, plus four others — each read against the five questions, one bounced back for an empty User Remark and resubmitted with its story. Review time: fourteen minutes. The count is noted as the layer's standing health figure.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the second law", "11", "<p>The manual layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the journal habit.</b> Symptom: hundreds of Journal Entries monthly; sales and purchases journalised; the trading documents unused. Cause: manual-books fluency never transferred (Chapter 1). Fix: apply the document test to the whole traffic; migrate the biggest categories to their documents first — sales, then purchases, then payments; teach the transfer with respect; watch the monthly count as the recovery's measure.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — suspense parking.</b> Symptom: the balancing side nobody could find, parked in suspense; the account growing monthly. Cause: the one-sided instinct — posting what is known and warehousing the rest (Chapter 2). Fix: take the Difference field seriously (the other side always exists — find it or do not post), sweep suspense to zero monthly, and fix the classification gaps at source.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the tie-breaker journals.</b> Symptom: control accounts disagreeing with their party reports; a stock agreement diverged by a suspiciously round number; a tax account matching a filing but not its register. Cause: the forbidden list violated (Chapter 6). Fix: the General Ledger report on the protected accounts filtered to Voucher Type = Journal Entry (the fastest audit in the track), each hit reversed and re-routed through its proper instrument, the fence re-taught, the pen narrowed.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the storyless journal.</b> Symptom: entries whose User Remark is empty or says <i>adjustment</i>; reviews that stall on them; authors who cannot recall them. Cause: the remark rule unenforced (Chapter 2). Fix: require the story at entry, bounce storyless journals in the monthly review, and let templates carry pre-written remarks so routine traffic is never the problem.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the year-end journal storm.</b> Symptom: a quiet ledger all year, then fifty journals in the close week — accruals never run monthly, corrections hoarded. Cause: the manual layer run annually instead of monthly (Chapters 4, 7) — every month's statements wrong all year, corrected once, when nobody remembers anything. Fix: the recurring set on Auto Repeat and templates, corrections posted when found, the monthly review keeping the layer current.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state what the Journal Entry bypasses and why the damage is silent; walk the form's fields and their rules (Entry Type, Posting Date, the Accounting Entries table, the Difference field, References, User Remark); recite the lawful list and apply the two tests; perform the prepayment procedure with Auto Repeat and the accrual procedure with Reverse Journal Entry; state the four correction rules and choose between Cancel/Amend, the note machinery, and reverse-and-repost; recite the forbidden list with what each breaks and which fences the system enforces itself; and run the monthly review from the filtered list with the five questions.</p><p><b>The second law: the journal is the exception and the document is the rule — what a document can do, a document must do.</b> Module 3 takes the books to the bank: reconciliation.</p>"),
]

